{
  "dom_id": "step-q29546",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{4}^{x-2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={4}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/6879f4093eff2785ea9c011d14a803fa8bf66416d1e2d0eb72604e315992c231.png)\n\n- Horizontally shift it by $2$ units to the right to give $y={4}^{x-2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/ec74c258f65ee44191f29259357f089992e13a7113e6e777ff64214666c8c503.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{4}^{x-2}.$ The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253974/assets/6e1303b0cbf3688ba31a3be6d5c97a308f04775429e30db718c12c7e0f4df9f6.png)",
